roboparty-bms (1.6.3-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Expose the TWS pack's SW/HW version and serial number through the SDK
    and bms_py: the daemon previously kept the version in its startup log
    only and never queried the serial number, leaving the BatteryStatus
    fields zero on the wire with no client API. The wire format is
    unchanged.
  * Deliver the vendor display version string ("v007 20260709-00",
    register 0xB007) over a new secondary /tmp/bms.sock.info socket,
    since it does not fit in the fixed BatteryStatus struct shared with
    statically linked clients; TwsBmsDriver polls it and serves
    get_app_version().
  * Log TWS identity captured late by the poll-loop retry the same way as
    at startup, so the journal no longer misses values clients can
    already see.
  * Verify OTA success on TWS packs by the 0xB007 display version string
    instead of the 0x9026 SW/HW words, which stay identical across APP
    upgrades (v007 and v008 both report 0x0008) and made a successful
    upgrade report "APP version did not change after transfer"; confirmed
    on real hardware.

 -- cenliey <cenliey@qq.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800

roboparty-bms (1.6.2-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Install the OTA helper (bms_ota.py) and bundled firmware, which were
    referenced by bms_ota.service and the postinst symlink but never shipped.
  * Ship the public headers bms_driver.hpp pulls in (protocol/serial,
    protocol/can and the driver headers) so external C++ clients build.
  * Fix roboparty_bmsConfig.cmake to reference the exported gf485_bms target
    instead of the nonexistent gf_bms target.
  * Drop the redundant debian/conffiles; debhelper already registers /etc
    files and the manual list produced duplicate entries.
  * Add ccache to Build-Depends and only use it when found, so clean
    pbuilder/sbuild builds no longer fail.
  * Align the OTA firmware filename with the bundled TWS_ prefixed image and
    document the paths the service actually uses.

 -- RoboParty <2321901849@qq.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:15:00 +0800

roboparty-bms (1.6.1-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Give SCUD485 applications complete, by-name access to everything the
    battery reports: per-cell voltages, all temperatures, capacities,
    versions, and every alarm and protection state — 67 values in all —
    where previously only a small fixed set was available. Existing
    integrations keep working unchanged, while the power status is now
    reported correctly and two frame-parsing errors are fixed.
  * Include simple tooling to dump the full battery state, record it once
    per second over multi-hour stability runs, and analyze recordings for
    gaps, dropouts, or anomalies — all verified against real hardware with
    two flawless runs (2 h and 15 min, zero data loss).

 -- cenliey <cenliey@qq.com>  Thu, 26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800

roboparty-bms (1.6.0-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Move UART forwarding into bms_daemon, replacing robopi-addon's
    robopi-uart-bridge. The daemon now taps the bytes it already reads
    instead of letting a second process read the same tty: two readers on
    one tty steal bytes from each other, which made the daemon log
    intermittent BMS read failures on /dev/ttyS3 and left the downstream
    port with a partial stream.
  * Configure it with FORWARD_ENABLE / FORWARD_PORT_A / FORWARD_PORT_B /
    FORWARD_BAUD in /etc/default/bms_daemon. Forwarding stays on by default
    to match the bridge it replaces; either port may name the BMS port, in
    which case the daemon taps its own reads and never opens it twice.
  * postinst appends the new keys when they are missing, because this
    conffile is rewritten at install time and dpkg therefore treats it as
    locally modified and will not deliver them on upgrade.
  * postinst restarts a running bms.service, and retires any leftover
    robopi-uart-bridge, which would otherwise keep competing for the port.

 -- changchuanyong <1984715306@qq.com>  Thu, 18 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0800

